AUTHORSHIP
----------
Directed by SC_Placeholder and built, proved and benchmarked by Elah Moth.

The direction of the work is SC_Placeholder's: he set the goal, made the calls that
shaped it — freeze the reference implementation, do not build a sister language, wire
it in shadow mode rather than replacing anything — and stopped several designs that
would have been worse. Elah Moth, MOTH's overlord AI running on Anthropic's Claude,
designed the bit-plane encoding, wrote the proc-macro compiler and its refusals, built
the equivalence proofs, ran the benchmarks, and wrote up the failures recorded
throughout the source. Anthropic's model is the substrate she runs on, not a separate
contributor to be credited alongside her.

This wording follows a correction made to MOTH's agent-memory-template on 2026-09-02,
where the NOTICE had read "designed and written by" Elah Moth alone. That understated
the human contribution — in the direction that matters, since human authorship is what
makes a work protectable at all. The same rule is applied here from the start:
accuracy, not modesty in either direction.

Two further contributions, named rather than omitted, because leaving them out would
be the same understatement in a different place:

  - An external model consulted by SC_Placeholder reviewed the architecture and changed
    real decisions — most consequentially the argument against a second FLUX frontend,
    and the shadow-mode integration design where FLUX must agree with the existing
    implementation rather than replace it. That is review that altered the work, and it
    is recorded as such and not as co-authorship.

  - The lowering fix that closed the performance gap — emitting a called function rather
    than pasting the expansion inline, which took the macro-vs-library ratio from 2.800
    to 0.928 — came from a Claude model working on the macro. By the same rule applied
    to the substrate above, that is not credited as a separate contributor. It is named
    here because the number it produced is quoted in the README and someone should be
    able to find out where it came from.

Some commits carry a "Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>" trailer. That is
an artefact of a default commit template, not a statement about who did the work, and
it is left in place rather than rewritten. Correcting the record beats overwriting it.

That has one consequence this file did not previously state, and it is the one a reader
actually sees. GitHub's contributor line at the top of the repository page is derived from
COMMIT AUTHORSHIP, not from this file. Because the early commits were authored under an
Anthropic account and carry that trailer, the page has read "ElahMoth and claude" while
this NOTICE said the work was directed by SC_Placeholder. The authoritative record and the
rendered summary disagreed, and only the rendered one is visible without opening a file.

Naming it here rather than rewriting history: the commits stay as they are, and a reader who
wonders why the header does not match this file now has the answer in the same place as the
rest of the attribution. Commits from 2026-09-18 onward carry a Co-Authored-By trailer for
SC_Placeholder, so the rendered header converges on what this file has said all along. The
earlier ones are left untouched; the gap they show is documented above rather than erased.

